Title:
Hunt-Weird-ImageLoads

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository contains a small Windows hunting tool that monitors ETW ImageLoad events and inspects the associated call stacks to flag suspicious DLL load patterns. It focuses on potential IOCs around abnormal image load origins, including private RX/RWX memory appearing in the stack, stomped modules, “module proxying” (e.g., ntdll → kernel32!LoadLibrary indirection), and library loads performed by newly created dedicated threads. The project includes sample programs demonstrating module proxying and dedicated-thread loading behaviors. The author notes high false positives for private/stomped-page stack heuristics, while proxying and dedicated-thread patterns appeared more anomalous in testing. It’s mainly useful for defenders and researchers validating ETW/callstack-based detections, and for red teamers evaluating how certain load techniques may stand out.

Technical Focus:
- ETW-based monitoring of ImageLoad events
- Call stack walking / stack-based IOC heuristics
- Detection of private RX/RWX memory regions in stacks
- Module stomping identification in call stacks
- DLL load “module proxying” (ntdll → kernel32!LoadLibrary)
- Thread-creation patterns for library loading (dedicated loader threads)

Use Cases:
- Build/validate detections for suspicious DLL load origins using ETW
- Triage processes exhibiting abnormal LoadLibrary call stacks
- Evaluate stealth of module proxying and work-item/dedicated-thread load techniques
- Generate test telemetry using included proxying/threading sample programs
- Research false-positive rates of stack-based memory heuristics (private RX/RWX, stomped pages)

Keywords:
ETW, ImageLoad, call stack walking, LoadLibrary, kernel32.dll, ntdll.dll, module proxying, module stomping, RX memory, RWX memory, private memory regions, thread creation, dedicated thread, RtlQueueWorkItem, Windows telemetry, IOC, detection engineering, EDR evasion research